  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: A cool front continues to push southeast through the \nforecast area early this afternoon. Behind the front, elevated \nmoisture may continue to produce isolated showers or \nthunderstorms driven by a narrow corridor of vorticity sinking \nthrough central Minnesota. This activity is likely to remain \nelevated as it moves into south central Minnesota. Eventually \nit may become more surface based as it moves into northern Iowa.\nSecondary ACCAS or isolated thunderstorms further west into \nCentral South Dakota are expected to continue to struggle \nsoutheast. Elsewhere, moisture pooling ahead of the boundary \nalong with thermal compression ahead of the front will push heat\nindex values into the 100s along and south of I-90 this \nafternoon. Drier air advecting behind the passage of the front \nshould lead to dropping heat index values after 3pm from north \nto south, with all headlines on schedule to end by 7-8pm. \n\nThe southward progression of the boundary will continue into the \nevening reaching a line from Norfolk to Sioux City To Estherville by \n4-5pm. Soundings along the boundary continue to look very capped, \nwith much less forcing generally expected over the front. It's the \nACCAS over central SD that we'll need to keep an eye on early this \nevening, as it does have some potential to intensify slightly as it \nreaches Highway 20 and could become surface based.  IF upscale \ngrowth could happen, the combination of shear and instability \ncould signal some golf ball sized hail and 60 mph winds. Again \nthough, this looks like a VERY limited area in time and space \nthis evening.\n\nTONIGHT:  Dew points will crash overnight, signaling a return of \ncooler and drier air.  Overnight lows may fall well into the 60s by \ndaybreak Tuesday. \n\nTUESDAY: A beautiful day is anticipated on Tuesday with Canadian \nhigh pressure sinking southward. High temperatures only in the \nupper 70s to lower 80s with dew points in the 50s will signal a \ngreat. \n\nWEDNESDAY-FRIDAY:  The semi-northwest flow through the second half \nof the week will keep cooler temperatures in the region through the \nrest of the week.  Highs are expected to reach the lower 80s each \nday.  A weak perturbation in this flow pattern leaves the Central \nRockies early Wednesday.  Most guidance tracks this wave through \nNebraska, but a few solutions could bring light rain risks into mid-\nday Wednesday along and west of the James River valley.  A second \nwave arrives by Thursday night into Friday, but again, uncertainty \nin track keeps the risks for any precipitation less than 30% in most \nlocations along or west of I-29. \n\nSATURDAY-SUNDAY:  We'll again see rising temperatures by next \nweekend, as confidence is high in an expanding mid-lvl ridge through \nthe Plains. This ridge however will be more suppressed than the\none that has just left the region, signaling a shorter surge in\ntemperatures to the 90s/100s, but also allowing a ridge rider \nor two to move through the Plains by the end of the weekend.",
